    "lng": 100.5405,
    "radius": 300,
    "narration": "Lumpini Park is Bangkok's central oasis — 57 hectares of lakes, joggers, tai chi practitioners, monitor lizards sunning by the water, and paddleboats. Every morning at 05:30 Bangkokians exercise here, and every evening at 18:00 the national anthem plays over loudspeakers and everyone stops.",
    "description": "Bangkok's 57-hectare central park — monitor lizards, morning tai chi, paddleboats",
    "category": "Park",
    "detailedDescription": "The land was donated by King Rama VI in 1925 and named after the birthplace of the Buddha in Nepal. The park's Loch-like lake is home to large monitor lizards (water monitors, up to 2 metres) that lounge on the banks, utterly unafraid of humans. Early mornings are magical: retirees practice sword dancing, groups do aerobics to broadcast music, and older men play chess under the trees. Food vendors ring the park perimeter selling fresh coconut milk, corn cobs, and guava slices. The park connects to the Silom MRT station and is central to the business district.",
