    "narration": "The Great Synagogue on Dohány Street is the largest synagogue in Europe and second largest in the world, seating 3,000 worshippers. Built in Moorish Revival style in 1859, it stands as a monument to Budapest's once-thriving Jewish community.",
    "description": "Europe's largest synagogue (1859) — seats 3,000, stunning Moorish Revival twin towers",
    "category": "Religious Site",
    "detailedDescription": "Architect Ludwig Förster designed the stunning Moorish Revival synagogue with twin onion-dome towers and a blue-and-yellow striped facade. Inside, the rose window, gilded columns, and organ (on which Liszt and Saint-Saëns played) reflect the wealth of 19th-century Budapest's Jewish community. The attached Jewish Museum documents 2,000 years of Hungarian Jewish history. In the garden, a weeping willow memorial by Imre Varga lists the names of Budapest Jews murdered in the Holocaust — the willow's leaves each bear a name. A mass grave of 2,000 ghetto victims lies beneath the courtyard.",
