    "narration": "The Széchenyi Chain Bridge was the first permanent bridge connecting Buda and Pest when it opened in 1849. Counting on engineer William Tierney Clark's design and funded by Count István Széchenyi, it became a symbol of Hungarian progress and national identity.",
    "description": "Budapest's iconic 1849 suspension bridge — first permanent link between Buda and Pest",
    "category": "Landmark",
    "detailedDescription": "Count István Széchenyi, the great Hungarian reformer, was inspired to build a permanent bridge after being stranded in Buda by ice for weeks, unable to cross to attend his father's funeral. He commissioned English engineer William Tierney Clark (same designer as Hammersmith Bridge) and Scottish engineer Adam Clark to supervise construction. Destroyed by the retreating German army in 1945, it was rebuilt identically and reopened on its 100th anniversary, November 1949. The four lion statues at each end are subjects of an enduring Budapest legend: they are said to have no tongues — though they do, when inspected closely.",
