    "name": "Clarke Quay",
    "lat": 1.2907,
    "lng": 103.8463,
    "radius": 100,
    "narration": "Clarke Quay is Singapore's most vibrant riverside entertainment district, a cluster of restored 19th-century warehouses and shophouses on the Singapore River packed with restaurants, bars, and nightclubs. The G-MAX reverse bungee launching into the Singapore skyline is a local rite of passage.",
    "description": "Singapore's most vibrant riverside bar and dining district — restored 19th-c warehouses",
    "category": "District",
    "detailedDescription": "Named after Sir Andrew Clarke, Governor of Singapore in the 1870s who developed it as a trading hub, Clarke Quay's five historic blocks were restored in the 1990s. The riverside promenade connects directly to the Boat Quay financial district and Chinatown via the Singapore River Walk. Restaurants span all cuisines; the river-facing tables are the most coveted at sunset. The Attica nightclub hosted Singapore's first rave events in the 1990s. Long-tail boat tours of the Singapore River depart from the quay, passing Cavenagh Bridge (Singapore's last hand-drawn bridge) and Robertson Quay. The area is 10 minutes by taxi from the cruise terminal at HarbourFront.",
