    "narration": "Singapore's Chinatown is a UNESCO-tentative heritage district of shophouses built by the first Hokkien, Teochew, and Cantonese immigrants in the 1820s. The Buddha Tooth Relic Temple, the Sri Mariamman Hindu Temple, and a mosque sit within a few blocks — Singapore's multicultural identity in miniature.",
    "description": "Heritage shophouses, Buddha Tooth Temple, and all three major faiths within a few blocks",
    "category": "Historic District",
    "detailedDescription": "Sir Stamford Raffles demarcated the Chinese quarter in his 1822 town plan. The distinctive two-storey shophouses with their covered 'five-foot walkways' and painted shutters are Southeast Asian architectural treasures. The Buddha Tooth Relic Temple (2007) in Tang Dynasty style houses what is believed to be the left canine tooth of the Buddha from Myanmar; the rooftop garden and free entry make it worth visiting. Sri Mariamman Temple (1827) is Singapore's oldest Hindu temple. The Maxwell Food Centre and Tanjong Pagar Plaza hawker centres serve Hainanese chicken rice — Singapore's national dish — alongside char kway teow and chilli crab.",
