    "radius": 150,
    "narration": "Prague's Old Town Square has been the city's heart for 900 years. The Astronomical Clock on the Old Town Hall, installed in 1410, is the world's oldest still-functioning astronomical clock — every hour, the Twelve Apostles parade past its windows, and Death rings a bell.",
    "description": "900-year-old square with the world's oldest functioning astronomical clock (1410)",
    "category": "Historic Site",
    "detailedDescription": "The Orloj clock mechanism, created in 1410 by clockmaker Mikuláš of Kadaň, shows the position of the sun and moon across the zodiac, Old Bohemian time, Babylonian time, and sidereal time — remarkable for medieval engineering. The legend says that when Master Hanuš completed it, city councillors had him blinded so he couldn't build another; he allegedly crawled to the clock and stopped it, and it didn't work for 100 years. Every hour on the hour 10:00-20:00, the apostle parade draws crowds. The square is also home to the Gothic Týn Church, the Baroque St. Nicholas Church, and the Jan Hus Memorial.",
