    "narration": "Djurgården is Stockholm's royal island park, once a royal hunting ground and now home to the Vasa Museum, ABBA Museum, Skansen, and Gröna Lund amusement park — all within easy walking distance of each other on a peaceful green island.",
    "description": "Royal island park — Vasa Museum, ABBA Museum, Skansen, all within walking distance",
    "category": "Park",
    "detailedDescription": "Djurgården — 'Animal Garden' in Swedish — was reserved as a royal hunting ground from the 16th century. Today the island is Stockholm's cultural heartland, easily reached by ferry from Gamla Stan or by tram 7 from the city centre. In summer, the meadows and oak forests fill with Stockholmers cycling, picnicking, and rowing. Gröna Lund, Sweden's oldest amusement park (1883), sits at the island's western tip. The Nordic Museum, documenting Swedish folk culture, stands near the Vasa. The Rosendal Palace, a royal summer residence, is open for tours.",
