    "id": "prague_vysehrad",
    "cityId": "prague",
    "name": "Vyšehrad Fortress",
    "lat": 50.0651,
    "lng": 14.4185,
    "radius": 200,
    "narration": "Vyšehrad is a rocky promontory above the Vltava, home to Prague's oldest fortress and the cemetery where Czech national heroes are buried. Dvořák, Smetana, Mucha, and Čapek all rest here — the hillside is a pilgrimage site for Czech identity and national pride.",
    "description": "Prague's ancient fortress and national cemetery — Dvořák, Smetana, Mucha buried here",
    "category": "Castle",
    "detailedDescription": "Vyšehrad (High Castle) was the legendary seat of the Přemyslid dynasty from the 10th century and the mythological birthplace of Czech civilization — Princess Libuše prophesied Prague's greatness from this rock. The Romanesque rotunda of St. Martin dates from the 11th century. The twin-towered Gothic-Baroque Cathedral of Sts. Peter and Paul dominates the hilltop. The Vyšehrad Cemetery (Slavín) holds 600 distinguished Czechs: composer Antonín Dvořák's grave is marked with a harrow; Bedřich Smetana, playwright Karel Čapek, and Art Nouveau painter Alfons Mucha are also buried here. The park offers quiet views away from tourist crowds.",
