    "lat": 59.917,
    "lng": 10.7271,
    "radius": 100,
    "narration": "The Oslo Royal Palace stands at the top of Karl Johans Gate, Norway's main boulevard, its austere neoclassical facade looking down towards the parliament and the fjord. The changing of the guard at 13:30 daily is a Oslo tradition.",
    "description": "Norway's Royal Palace — daily changing of the guard at 13:30, open for summer tours",
    "category": "Palace",
    "detailedDescription": "King Karl Johan commissioned the palace in 1824 and it was completed in 1849 in a simple neoclassical style reflecting Norwegian egalitarianism — there is no protective gate around it; the public walks the gardens freely. The equestrian statue of Karl Johan in the forecourt was cast in Paris. The palace was the headquarters of Norwegian Nazi collaborator Vidkun Quisling during WWII. Guided tours (summer only) reveal state apartments with crystal chandeliers, Norwegian tapestries, and portraits of the royal family dating to the Bernadotte dynasty. The palace park is Oslo's most popular lunchtime escape.",
