    "narration": "Malá Strana — Little Side or Lesser Town — is the Baroque neighbourhood between Prague Castle and Charles Bridge, filled with palaces, garden courtyards, and the magnificent St. Nicholas Church with its 79-metre dome visible from across the city.",
    "description": "Prague's Baroque neighbourhood — garden palaces, St. Nicholas dome, cobblestone lanes",
    "category": "Historic District",
    "detailedDescription": "Established in 1257 by King Přemysl Otakar II for German merchants, Malá Strana was rebuilt in Baroque style after the 1541 great fire. The district is a UNESCO World Heritage Site of unsurpassed Baroque architecture. St. Nicholas Church (1703–1755) with its 79-metre dome and 20-metre frescoes by Franz Xaver Palko is among Europe's finest Baroque churches. The Wallenstein Palace garden (open free in summer) was Europe's first Baroque garden and contains bronze statues cast in the Netherlands. The area is home to dozens of Czech and foreign embassies in restored palaces, and the atmospheric café Kavárna Slavia on the embankment.",
