    "radius": 200,
    "narration": "Little India is Singapore's most vibrant and aromatic neighbourhood — streets of flower garland sellers, sari shops, Tamil restaurants serving banana-leaf rice, and the Sri Veeramakaliamman Temple festooned with 21 gopuram towers of hand-painted deities.",
    "description": "Singapore's most aromatic neighbourhood — flower garlands, banana-leaf rice, gopuram temple",
    "category": "Historic District",
    "detailedDescription": "Little India developed from the 1820s around the cattle trade and grew into a community of Tamil, Telugu, and Punjabi immigrants. Serangoon Road is the main artery, lined with goldsmiths, textile shops, and money changers. Sri Veeramakaliamman Temple (1881) is dedicated to the fierce goddess Kali; its elaborately painted gopuram (entrance tower) is covered in hundreds of statues. The Mustafa Centre operates 24 hours selling everything from saris to electronics. The tekka Centre wet market in the morning sells fresh tropical produce and flowers for temple offerings. Deepavali (Festival of Lights) transforms the district into a riot of illuminations.",
